  "uid": "426449.,jLUD9GGuRwsAimBaz7nPLWx3ZChHvE,Xlbnfayzqgo=",
  "from_addr": "Mike Allen <mike@axios.com>",
  "subject": "⏳ Axios Finish Line: Longevity rebellion",
  "date": "Thu, 19 Nov 2026 21:29:24 -0500",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🌇 Plus: Cali sunset | Thursday, November 19, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Nov 19, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Thanks for joining Finish Line this week.\n\n Smart Brevity™ count: 483 words ... 2 mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: The people who don&#x27;t want to live forever \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n The pursuit of longevity is a multibillion-dollar industry. But there's a growing rejection of longevity culture: Doctors, researchers and ordinary Americans are questioning whether continually extending life is a worthy goal.\n\n Why it matters: The final years of life can come with declining health and independence. 📈 By the numbers: Americans can expect to live to 79, on average.\n\n But healthspan, the years we live in full health, is about 65. So the average person spends their final 14 years in declining health, The Wall Street Journal reports ( gift link ). Case in point: Dr. Zeke Emanuel, a 69-year-old oncologist and medical ethicist, has said he'd be happy to die at 75 , and he'll refuse life-extending medical interventions after that age.\n\n In \" Eat Your Ice Cream : Six Simple Rules for a Long and Healthy Life,\" out early this year, Emanuel argues that society's obsession with longevity puts too much emphasis on adding years to the end, and too little emphasis on living fully now. The key to living a good life \"doesn't require a lot of money, and it's not following a lot of the stupid things that you read, that you hear on Instagram, TikTok, wherever,\" Emanuel tells The New York Times ( gift link ). \n\n He focuses on getting the pillars of sleep, exercise and diet mostly right, but says the most important ingredient for a long and rewarding life is prioritizing relationships with others. 🔭 Zoom out: Emanuel is part of a growing chorus of people embracing aging.\n\n Dietitian Deb Benfield tells the N.Y. Times that longevity culture is becoming a new form of diet culture, particularly for women, who are bombarded with messages that their aging bodies are problems to fix. In her book, \" Unapologetic Aging : How to Mend and Nourish Your Relationship with Your Body,\" she encourages women to embrace their changing bodies rather than spend time and money trying to look and feel young. Peter Geis, 83, told the WSJ that he cared for his wife as she battled Alzheimer's and watched his father spend his final days intubated in a hospital. Now facing his own health issues, he's told his children he's ready to go. He says he hopes his final days, whether tomorrow or in 10 years, come without a prolonged hospital stay. ❤️ Parting thought: \"Life is beautiful, and I adore it,\" Cecelia Poole, 73, a retired nurse in hospice with advanced heart failure, told the WSJ. Though a device has helped keep her heart beating, she's declined further interventions that might extend her life without improving its quality . \n\n \"I have chosen to accept, with grace, that a life well-lived can also end with dignity.\" \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n Break through the noise with Axios \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Reach execs and business leaders with Axios.\n\n\r\n We'll help you tell your story in the right way:\n\n\r\n \r\n We'll distill your brand's message into its most effective form with Smart Brevity. \r\n No clutter, no filler — just clean, smart and effective. \r\n \r\n Contact us to learn more. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🌊 Pic to go! \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: K.C. Alfred/The San Diego Union-Tribune via Getty Images A woman walks in the rain at sunset in La Jolla, Calif., earlier this week.\n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
