cc1d5\"><img src=\"https://link.axios.com/img/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94asw78h.71ge/2839d499.gif\" alt=\"\" border=\"0\" /></a></td>\r\n<td style=\"padding-right:3.5%\" align=\"right\"> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.328478/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL25ld3NsZXR0ZXJzL2F4aW9zLWZpbmlzaC1saW5lLTFkODVmYmRiLWJmNTktNGFhMS05ZDZhLTcyYTdhNzkwZjcxNy5odG1sP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB2dae7cbd\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"b\" style=\"color:#222;font-size:13px;font-family:'gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;text-decoration:underline\">View in browser</a> </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td height=\"30\" style=\"height:30px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>  <tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1.3\"> </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td height=\"16\" style=\"height:16px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1\">\r\n<span class=\"s\" style=\"font-size:44px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#008dc8;letter-spacing:-1px;line-height:1\"> Axios Finish Line </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1.5;padding-bottom:35px;padding-top:10px\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#222;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">By  Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei  <span style=\"margin:0 6px\">&middot;</span> Nov 19, 2026 </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.75;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;padding:0 3.5%\"><p><strong>Welcome back!</strong> Thanks for joining Finish Line this week.</p><ul><li><em>Smart Brevity™ count: 483 words ... 2 mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern.</em></li></ul></td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: The people who don&#x27;t want to live forever </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/WvnDCf909LxkCnM0GYNJ1RijW7s=/0x0:1920x1080/1920x1080/2026/06/17/1781722064109.jpeg\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Illustration of an hourglass that repeats so the sand never runs out. \"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>The pursuit of longevity</strong> is a multibillion-dollar industry. But there's a growing rejection of longevity culture: Doctors, researchers and ordinary Americans are questioning whether continually extending life is a worthy goal.</p><ul><li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> The final years of life can come with declining health and independence. </li></ul><p><strong>📈 By the numbers:</strong> Americans can expect to live to 79, on average.</p><ul><li><strong>But </strong><strong><em>healthspan, </em></strong>the years we live in full health, is about 65. So the average person spends their final 14 years in declining health, <em>The Wall Street Journal reports (</em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.\" target=\"_blank\"><em>gift link</em></a><em>).</em></li></ul><p><strong>Case in point:</strong> Dr. Zeke Emanuel, a 69-year-old oncologist and medical ethicist, has said he'd be <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.328478/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cudGhlYXRsYW50aWMuY29tL21hZ2F6aW5lL2FyY2hpdmUvMjAxNC8xMC93aHktaS1ob3BlLXRvLWRpZS1hdC03NS8zNzkzMjkvP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBccaea389\" target=\"_blank\">happy to die at 75</a>, and he'll refuse life-extending medical interventions after that age.</p><ul><li><strong>In \"</strong><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.328478/aHR0cHM6Ly93d25vcnRvbi5jb20vYm9va3MvOTc4MTMyNDExNzUzNz91dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB647b176f\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Eat Your Ice Cream</strong></a><strong>: </strong>Six Simple Rules for a Long and Healthy Life,\" out early this year, Emanuel argues that society's obsession with longevity puts too much emphasis on adding years to the end, and too little emphasis on living fully <em>now.</em></li></ul><p><strong>The key to living </strong>a good life \"doesn't require a lot of money, and it's not following a lot of the stupid things that you read, that you hear on Instagram, TikTok, wherever,\" <em>Emanuel tells The New York Times (<a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.328478/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cubnl0aW1lcy5jb20vMjAyNi8xMS8xOS93ZWxsL2FudGktbG9uZ2V2aXR5LWluZmx1ZW5jZXJzLmh0bWw_dW5sb2NrZWRfYXJ0aWNsZV9jb2RlPTEuUmxFLmtEbC0uWjFFNjh2MVZLVU5sJnNtaWQ9cmUtc2hhcmUmdXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB8a06bf85\" target=\"_blank\">gift link</a>).</em></p><ul><li><strong>He focuses on </strong>getting the pillars of sleep, exercise and diet mostly right, but says the <em>most</em> important ingredient for a long and rewarding life is prioritizing relationships with others.</li></ul><p><strong>🔭 Zoom out: </strong>Emanuel is part of a growing chorus of people embracing aging.</p><ul><li><strong>Dietitian Deb Benfield</strong> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.328478/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cubnl0aW1lcy5jb20vMjAyNi8xMS8xOS93ZWxsL2FudGktbG9uZ2V2aXR5LWluZmx1ZW5jZXJzLmh0bWw_c21pZD11cmwtc2hhcmUmdXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aCfd518e38\" target=\"_blank\">tells</a> the N.Y. Times that longevity culture is becoming a new form of diet culture, particularly for women, who are bombarded with messages that their aging bodies are problems to fix. In her book, \"<a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.328478/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZGVicmFiZW5maWVsZC5jb20vYm9vaz91dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBf77c6642\" target=\"_blank\">Unapologetic Aging</a>: How to Mend and Nourish Your Relationship with Your Body,\" she encourages women to embrace their changing bodies rather than spend time and money trying to look and feel young.<strong> </strong></li><li><strong>Peter Geis, 83,</strong> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.\" target=\"_blank\">told</a> the WSJ that he cared for his wife as she battled Alzheimer's and watched his father spend his final days intubated in a hospital. Now facing his own health issues, he's told his children he's ready to go. He says he hopes his final days, whether tomorrow or in 10 years, come without a prolonged hospital stay.</li></ul><p><strong>❤️ Parting thought:</strong> \"Life is beautiful, and I adore it,\" Cecelia Poole, 73, a retired nurse in hospice with advanced heart failure, told the WSJ. Though a device has helped keep her heart beating, she's declined further interventions that might extend her life without improving its quality<em>.</em></p><ul><li><strong>\"I have chosen to accept, </strong>with grace, that a life well-lived can also end with dignity.\"</li></ul></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.328478/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtMWQ4NWZiZGItYmY1OS00YWExLTlkNmEtNzJhN2E3OTBmNzE3Lmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB16870dd1\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-facebook-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Share on Facebook\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.328478/aHR0cHM6Ly90d2l0dGVyLmNvbS9pbnRlbnQvdHdlZXQ_\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-x-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Tweet this Story\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48531617.\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-linkedin-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Post to LinkedIn\"></a>  <a href=\"mailto:?subject=From Axios: 1 big thing: The people who don&#x27;t want to live forever&body=teruyuki%40hh.iij4u.or.jp%20has%20shared%20an%20Axios%20story%20with%20you%3A%0A%0A1 big thing: The people who don&#x27;t want to live forever%0Ahttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.axios.com%2Fnewsletters%2Faxios-finish-line-1d85fbdb-bf59-4aa1-9d6a-72a7a790f717.html%3Fchunk%3D1%26utm_term%3Demshare%23story1\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img